      "role": "user",
      "content": "nondestructive testing sounds pretty intense for a residential job. i don't exactly have radiography or ultrasonic gear in my truck. is there anything more low-tech i can do to find those internal flaws or \"hidden\" weak spots? like, is there any specific way the wood looks or feels when there's a defect lurking deep inside that isn't visible on the outside?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right, non-destructive testing can be a bit high-tech for a residential job. But, you can still use some low-tech methods to detect internal flaws. For example, when inspecting the timber, look for signs of \"goblin's knot\" – a knot that's hidden deep within the wood, causing it to twist or curve in an unusual way. This can be a sign of internal decay or rot.\n\nAnother method is to use a \"tap test\" – gently tap the beam with a hammer or mallet, listening for any unusual sounds or vibrations. This can help you detect internal defects like cracks or voids that might not be visible on the surface.\n\nYou can also use a \"screw test\" – insert a screw or nail into the beam, and then pull it out slowly. If the screw or nail is difficult to remove or seems to be stuck, it could indicate internal decay or rot.\n\nWhen handling the timber, pay attention to its \"feel\" – if it feels spongy or soft to the touch, it could be a sign of internal moisture or decay. And, of course, always look for any visible signs of insect damage, like tiny holes or tunnels.\n\nIt's also worth noting that",
      "source": "target",
